  "notes": "Scope: the 35 files of the go-core chunk per the lane manifest (acdsl CLI + verifier, configserver cmd incl. windows install, internal/acdsl, internal/config, internal/contextdocs, internal/evals, internal/peek, internal/repos/registry.go, internal/routines, internal/sessions, internal/shell, internal/skills), diffed b4981b88..e5245d08. Out of scope for this lane (reviewed by sibling chunks, not re-walked here): internal/server/* HTTP handlers and templates (bootstrap.go, repos.go, sessions.go, profilesettings.go, presentation.go, etc.) — these are where the configserver's request-facing attack surface (the actual HTTP routes mutating repos.json, routines, sessions) lives, and internal/repos/registry.go's Repo.Label field (new in this diff) is rendered by templates in that other chunk; I could not verify there that Label is HTML-escaped on output. Flagging as a cross-chunk item for the station rather than chasing it outside this chunk's files. No quantifiable, reproducible exploit was found inside the go-core chunk's own files: shell.Run/RunSync always exec with an argv array (never a shell string), so none of the new call sites (writeInstallEnv, Fingerprint, WorktreeCounts, RunSync in install_windows.go) are command-injectable; the Windows PowerShell command builders in sched_windows.go and install_windows.go interpolate only values that are regex/pattern-constrained before reaching them (repo paths generated by Go itself, routine labels constrained to plist filenames or to internal/routines.Duplicate's `^[a-z0-9-]+$` newName check), so no attacker-controlled string reaches the single-quoted PowerShell literals. internal/repos/registry.go's Repo.Validate was hardened in this diff (rejects \".\", \"..\", \"default\", \"archived\", and the name pattern already excludes \"/\"), closing off path-segment traversal through repo Name. The configserver's peek-mcp identity check (main.go's new peekIdentity/startPeek) is a real improvement over the prior blind reuse of any listener on the port; it is still a loopback TOCTOU (an attacker already running arbitrary code on the same box as another local profile could race the bind or spoof /healthz) but that is a pre-existing, documented, accepted shared-loopback condition, not a new regression, and exploiting it requires local code execution the attacker already has — not independently reproducible here as a standalone finding per ACTION-REVIEW-SEC-002/003. No secrets, credentials, or payment-like payloads are logged anywhere in this chunk (ACTION-REVIEW-LOG-004/005 n/a — no such logging exists).",
